{
  "question": "Does forcing automatic downward substitution materially affect the economic comparisons?",
  "assumptions": "Round74: isolate the fixed automatic-substitution restriction.\n\nFreeze EACH input chosen for the three baseline classes in round73, then allow\noptional downward allocation. Only that decision rule is relaxed. This is a\nfixed-input sensitivity test, not reoptimization of input under the new model.\nSetup decisions precede success. B<D2 states cannot rework, so optional\nsubstitution cost bands can be merged with setup trades without future leakage.\n",
  "structural_argument": "Optional downward allocation and pre-success setup in one service cover.\n\nThis is a separate extension of the frozen automatic-substitution baseline.\nUnder the surplus-only rework rule, B<D2 implies no rework. Substitution\ntradeoffs therefore occur in states disjoint from positive-return setup choices.\nDropping partial substitutions that cannot complete the whole order increases\nprofit without reducing the full-order service objective. Completable shortages\nk=D2-B cost30*k; pre-success setup trades lose F-G and gain binomial-tail b.\nCombine both sets in one weighted cover, retaining the original decision times.\n",
